{"reference":"Matthew 23","book":"Matthew","chapter":23,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the Woes Carefully","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Matthew 23’s sharp denunciations of scribes and Pharisees take place within Jewish worlds shaped by Scripture, debate about the law, shared practices, and institutions. The chapter’s polemic is intense, but it concludes with a lament over Jerusalem (vv. 37–39). Reading both elements together helps prevent the rebukes from being treated as a judgment on Judaism as a whole; the exact relationship between the Gospel’s later conflicts and its portrayal here remains disputed.","verse_refs":["Matthew 23:1-39"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_023_de83a8664248a6702da9e6c5"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}